WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.599
Before planning a migration,

00:00:01.599 --> 00:00:05.723
teams need to understand what cryptography is doing in each place.

00:00:05.723 --> 00:00:11.721
Key establishment is about helping two parties arrive at shared secret material over an untrusted network.

00:00:11.721 --> 00:00:16.594
A key encapsulation mechanism, or KEM, is one way to support that role.

00:00:16.594 --> 00:00:17.244
Conceptually,

00:00:17.244 --> 00:00:23.242
one party creates protected key material that another party can recover with the right private information,

00:00:23.242 --> 00:00:27.741
allowing both sides to derive a shared secret for later symmetric encryption.

00:00:27.741 --> 00:00:29.990
Digital signatures play a different role.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:34.489
A signature lets a verifier check that data was signed by the

00:00:34.489 --> 00:00:38.987
holder of a private key and that the signed data has not

00:00:38.987 --> 00:00:39.737
been modified.

00:00:39.737 --> 00:00:43.111
That supports authentication, integrity, software update trust, code signing,

00:00:43.111 --> 00:00:46.485
certificate authorities, secure boot chains, identity tokens, document signing,

00:00:46.485 --> 00:00:47.609
and device enrollment.

00:00:47.609 --> 00:00:49.858
Signatures do not automatically provide confidentiality.

00:00:49.858 --> 00:00:53.232
They answer questions about origin and integrity, not secrecy.

00:00:53.232 --> 00:00:57.356
These roles appear in many systems that practitioners use every day.

00:00:57.356 --> 00:01:01.854
TLS uses key establishment and authentication to protect web sessions and APIs.

00:01:01.854 --> 00:01:05.228
VPNs and SSH use public-key mechanisms for remote access.

00:01:05.228 --> 00:01:07.852
Software updates and packages rely on signatures.

00:01:07.852 --> 00:01:09.727
Certificates bind keys to identities.

00:01:09.727 --> 00:01:11.976
Secure email, device authentication, identity platforms,

00:01:11.976 --> 00:01:17.974
and firmware signing all depend on cryptographic roles that may have to change at different speeds.

00:01:17.974 --> 00:01:21.723
Replacing one algorithm is therefore rarely a simple find-and-replace operation.

00:01:21.723 --> 00:01:26.596
A KEM change can affect protocol negotiation, message size, performance, and library support.

00:01:26.596 --> 00:01:29.970
A signature change can affect certificate formats, chain sizes,

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.344
hardware security module behavior, storage, logging, and verification code.

00:01:33.344 --> 00:01:38.217
The lesson is practical: identify the role first, then choose the migration path.

00:01:38.217 --> 00:01:43.940
A team that treats all cryptography as one category will miss dependencies and create avoidable surprises.
